    doc+='<section id="rank"><h2>Necessary rank projection: smaller graph, same early search</h2><p>For zero-based row '+r'\(j\)'+' a placed output must have minimum complete-hypergraph derivation rank at most '+r'\(j\)'+'. Each selected strict-prior source row must likewise be at least the required premise rank. Every full retained candidate was classified through the complete shared graph. All eligible keys remain; no target backcone or selected path is used. Independent full-universe reconstruction and a strict-prior safety proof preserve exactly the same successful finite word language.</p>'+table(['Inventory','Retained placements → rank eligible','Excluded keys'],[(a['lane'],str(a['complete_full_retained_universe'])+' → '+str(a['exact_rank_eligible_universe']),a['excluded']) for a in json.loads((OUT/'rank-audit.json').read_text())['variants']])+table(['Fresh rank run','Outcome','Attempts','Search seconds','Model seconds','Placements'],[(o['id'],o['status'],o['attempts'],sec(o['search_seconds']),sec(o['model_setup_seconds']),o['placements']) for o in rank_search['observations']])+'<p>All four remain search-budget unknowns. All 20,004 new nodes were independently replayed. The original and projected runs have identical early tree decisions/counts within each inventory at this attempt boundary; the representation reduction has not demonstrated better search ordering.</p></section>'
    doc+='<section id="forward-control"><h2>A matched ordinary forward-chaining control</h2><p>After all eight GCTS unknowns were preserved and audited, a separately frozen ordinary forward algorithm traversed every native-admitted retained hyperedge to the complete fixed point. It kept the first ready edge in unchanged catalog order for each newly reachable formula, then reconstructed a strictly ordered proof DAG. No human derivation, chosen induction variable or target path was provided. This algorithm is a control, not GCTS, a GCTS proposal, or RL.</p>'+table(['Inventory','All hyperedges','Reached formulas','Edge scans','Extraction seconds','Word rows','Fits 12 rows'],[(o['lane'],o['retained_interfaces'],o['complete_closure_formulas'],o['edge_scans'],sec(o['extraction_seconds']),o['word_length'],o['same_12_row_boundary_fits']) for o in forward['observations']])+'<p>Both lanes produce byte-identical 11-row requests. The zero-left inventory theorem is absent from their target dependency. This is concrete evidence that the declared finite grammar can produce a target candidate while blind GCTS struggles with ordering at the recorded budget. It gives no inventory benefit here and no RL advantage. The point encoding below also independently verifies an exact 12-row embedding with one irrelevant zero-input filler.</p><p class="unknown">Whole native certification remains unknown. The 11-row request and its mechanically expanded 79-command / shared 67-command primitive version both reach unchanged heap band <code>26</code>, head <code>73,178</code> equal to capacity <code>73,178</code>. Independent complete parser input/output/step/FNV replay reproduces both limits. Local admission and host logical acceptance do not replace unfinished whole native checking.</p>'+table(['Whole control variant','Commands','Outcome','Fresh native seconds','Microsteps'],[(q['label'] if 'label' in q else str(j),len(q['request']['proof']),q['result']['status'],sec(q['seconds']),number(q['result']['micro_steps'])) for j,q in enumerate(forward_records)])+'<p>The second inventory uses the exact first-inventory native request results, with those historical costs disclosed rather than charged twice.</p></section>'
